What I’d correct in the premise
Store distribution and Microsoft’s commerce system are separate choices. For non-game apps, Microsoft lets developers use their own commerce system and keep 100% of the revenue. The 15% only applies if you sell through Microsoft’s payment platform. Individual developer registration is also now free, so listing costs nothing. You can put TotalRecalls in the Store, get the trusted install path, and take payment through your own system (Paddle, Stripe, a license key and so on). You should confirm the current Store policy wording on this and on how the free-to-paid unlock must work before you build around it. 
TechCrunch

Your reading of Azure signing is right. Microsoft’s documentation says that signing with Azure Artifact Signing does not provide instant SmartScreen trust. It also says EV certificates no longer bypass SmartScreen, so there is no purchasable shortcut. Developers reported fresh warnings through 2026, including after a March–April 2026 intermediate CA transition, so even established publishers get hit. Microsoft says new apps outside the Store typically need several weeks of download history to build reputation. Apps installed from the Store are re-signed by Microsoft and users never see a SmartScreen warning. 
