Your options
Option	Warning at install?	Your cost per sale	Main drawback
Direct download, signed (Artifact Signing, about $10/month)	Yes, for weeks or more	Payment processor only	Warning hits every early user, and every new build can reset file reputation
Store with Microsoft commerce	No	15%	Costs the most per sale
Store with your own commerce	No	Processor fees only	More setup, and you must pass Store certification
Hybrid: Store plus a signed direct download	No in the Store, yes on the direct download	Mixed	Two release paths to maintain
Artifact Signing is open to individuals in Canada, so you qualify from Montréal. Your own checkout fees would be roughly 3% plus 30¢ on Stripe or about 5% plus 50¢ with a merchant-of-record service like Paddle, though check current rates. On a $24 sale that’s about $1.00 to $1.70, versus $3.60 at 15%. On a $49 sale it’s about $1.70 to $2.95, versus $7.35. The difference is real but small next to losing buyers at the warning screen. A merchant-of-record service also handles sales tax and VAT for you, which matters when you’re solo.

My recommended course
Make the Store your primary storefront and the destination for your website’s main download button. Trial users and buyers get no warning. Your launch is the moment of highest attention, so the first impression has to be clean.
Start with your own commerce if you can integrate it cleanly, with Microsoft’s 15% as the fallback. At your price points, 15% is painful on the $49 Founder’s Pass but tolerable on the $24 downloader. Don’t let payment plumbing delay launch. Paying 15% for a month is cheaper than a late launch.
Sign everything anyway. Artifact Signing is about $10/month. Even though it won’t clear SmartScreen immediately, an unsigned file starts from zero reputation with every update, while signed files build publisher reputation over time. That makes your direct download progressively less scary.
Keep the direct download as a secondary channel for people who prefer it, with a short note on the download page explaining the “More info → Run anyway” step. Ship fewer, larger releases, since each new file hash restarts reputation. If you get flagged, submit the file to Microsoft’s Defender analysis portal for review.
Start Store certification now. It takes days and may need a revision round, and you don’t want it blocking launch.
Risks to check before committing
Content policy. I don’t know exactly what the downloader fetches or from where. Microsoft’s Store certification can reject apps that download third-party content in ways that violate other sites’ terms. Read the Store policies against that before you invest in packaging.
Packaging constraints. Your plan for a ZIP distribution with in-app drop install of SSE modules may conflict with MSIX, because installed package files are read-only. Modules would need to live in the app’s data folder, or ship as Store updates or add-ons. The latter fits the Store’s own add-on model, and may suit your staged 6-week drops better.
Updates. Store updates go through certification, so urgent hotfixes are slower than a direct download.
